---
title: Spiky actors
sidebar:
  order: 5
---

Spiky actors hurt the player or their partner when they use contact attacks. Event flags on the actor's parts control which attacks the spikes hurt.

The Spike Shield badge protects the player from top, front, and Spiny spikes.

## Top spikes

Set `ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_SPIKY_TOP` on a part's `.eventFlags` to hurt attackers that jump on the actor:

```c
ActorPartBlueprint ActorParts[] = {
    {
        .flags = ACTOR_PART_FLAG_PRIMARY_TARGET,
        .index = PRT_MAIN,
        // ...
        .eventFlags = ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_SPIKY_TOP,
    },
};
```

Cleft and Spike Top use top spikes.

## Front spikes

Set `ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_SPIKY_FRONT` to hurt attackers that use any contact attack other than a jump, such as a hammer. Attacks with `DAMAGE_TYPE_NO_CONTACT` don't touch front spikes.

```c
.eventFlags = ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_SPIKY_FRONT,
```

Spear Guy uses front spikes for its spear.

You can combine both flags to make an actor spiky from all angles:

```c
.eventFlags = ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_SPIKY_TOP | ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_SPIKY_FRONT,
```

## Spiny spikes

`ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_ALT_SPIKY` hurts the player when they jump on the actor, but not when they use the hammer. It hurts a partner that uses any contact attack, unless the actor has `ACTOR_FLAG_UPSIDE_DOWN`. Spiny and Pokey use it.

```c
.eventFlags = ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_ALT_SPIKY,
```

## The spike taunt

When the player or their partner gets hurt by attacking a spiky actor, the actor receives `EVENT_SPIKE_TAUNT`. You can handle it in `EVS_HandleEvent` to play a taunt animation:

```c
CaseEq(EVENT_SPIKE_TAUNT)
    Call(SetAnimation,
        ACTOR_SELF, PRT_MAIN, ANIM_Cleft_Laugh)
    Wait(30)
```

## Toggling spikes at runtime

You can turn spikes on and off at runtime with `SetPartEventBits`, such as for an actor that loses its spikes while it's flipped over:

```c
// turn off top spikes when the actor is flipped over
Call(SetPartEventBits,
    ACTOR_SELF, PRT_MAIN,
    ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_SPIKY_TOP, false)

// turn them back on when it gets back up
Call(SetPartEventBits,
    ACTOR_SELF, PRT_MAIN,
    ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_SPIKY_TOP, true)
```

## Flipable actors

A part with `ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_FLIPABLE` can be flipped over by jump, POW, and quake attacks. When one of those attacks hits it, the actor receives `EVENT_FLIP_TRIGGER` in place of `EVENT_HIT`. Combine the flag with spiky flags to make an enemy that's only vulnerable when flipped:

```c
.eventFlags = ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_ALT_SPIKY | ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_FLIPABLE,
```

When you handle `EVENT_FLIP_TRIGGER`, you usually:

- Turn off the spiky flags.
- Swap to a different defense table with lower defense.
- Change the actor's animations.
- Set `ACTOR_FLAG_FLIPPED` on the actor.

For a flipable spiky actor that gets back up, see `battle/actor/spiny.c`.

To flip an actor with other attacks, check the attack's element in `EVS_HandleEvent` and flip it yourself. Cleft doesn't have `ACTOR_EVENT_FLAG_FLIPABLE`. It uses `GetLastElement` to flip over when an explosion hits it:

```c
CaseOrEq(EVENT_HIT_COMBO)
CaseOrEq(EVENT_HIT)
    Call(GetLastElement, LVar0)
    Switch(LVar0)
        CaseFlag(DAMAGE_TYPE_BLAST)
            ExecWait(EVS_FlipOver)
        CaseDefault
            // play the usual hit reaction
    EndSwitch
EndCaseGroup
```

For the full example, see `battle/actor/cleft.c`.
